      "text": [
        {
          "id": "title",
          "visible": true,
          "kicker": "#0 · the Skenfrith dialogue source; the Q27 Mother-flow correction governs",
          "title": "The encounter at Skenfrith",
          "italic": "The ambiguity has an exact place: what appears to the viewer can initiate interpretation while remaining answerable to the object — no lunar or fertility symbolism is assigned to a supposed medieval rabbit.",
          "body": "",
          "x": 0.04,
          "y": 0.05,
          "width": 560,
          "size": 30,
          "align": "left"
        },
        {
          "id": "body1",
          "visible": true,
          "kicker": "",
          "title": "",
          "italic": "",
          "body": "The Mother flow begins with a body received, a body bearing another, and the receiving body itself returned. Taylor's encounter with the cope at St Bridget's in Skenfrith gathers the images: Mary being lifted, figures perceived as rabbits beside her, six-winged seraphic forms, wheels, eagles with two heads. The dialogue is a conversation with an assistant whose proposed readings have different authority from Taylor's corrections; the assistant's identification of angels invokes",
          "x": 0.04,
          "y": 0.47,
          "width": 620,
          "size": 14,
          "align": "left",
          "w": 520
        },
        {
          "id": "body2",
          "visible": true,
          "kicker": "",
          "title": "",
          "italic": "",
          "body": "an unnamed description — without a public object witness it remains a lead. The later quilt expressly re-keys the frame: the visit was personal; the essay-usable material is the Mother flow, Assumption and chiasm — no castle history climbs to a theological conclusion. Mary remains a particular mother; the Assumption belongs to a later doctrinal history, not another Gospel episode; Jung's interpretation and Taylor's chiasm are further operations meeting through the same figure.\n\nOccurrences: the lifted Mary and the rabbit/angel ambiguity (#0); the two wheels and six wings (#0, developed at #3); the two-headed eagle (#0, corrected at #4).",
          "x": 0.6,
          "y": 0.47,
          "width": 505,
          "size": 14,
          "align": "left"
        }
      ],
