      "text": [
        {
          "id": "title",
          "visible": true,
          "kicker": "#3–#4 · Taylor's explicit corrections (human-amplified: yes); A11/C49; A16; A13/C50",
          "title": "Wheels, wings and the eagle's signed power",
          "italic": "The corrections demonstrate authorship governing pattern: a pleasing reading is revised against the native relations, and the eagle's shared body is asked which operation it carries.",
          "body": "",
          "x": 0.04,
          "y": 0.05,
          "width": 560,
          "size": 30,
          "align": "left"
        },
        {
          "id": "body1",
          "visible": true,
          "kicker": "",
          "title": "",
          "italic": "",
          "body": "The two wheels give the crossing a second form: the full sequence 0/1 → 1/0 → (0/1)/(1/0) — a first orientation meets its inverse and their relation becomes explicit; the outer slash retains the orientations in operative self-difference, dividing no numerical quantities. As encountered image, a wheel permits changing orientation while its whole form persists; two wheels can bear conjugate orientations without becoming two independent sources. The seraphic forms give orientation a sixfold articulation: one sixfold and its primed counterpart, each retaining the two blocks — while the dialogue's first attempt at vertical",
          "x": 0.04,
          "y": 0.47,
          "width": 620,
          "size": 14,
          "align": "left",
          "w": 520
        },
        {
          "id": "body2",
          "visible": true,
          "kicker": "",
          "title": "",
          "italic": "",
          "body": "pairing is explicitly corrected: 2↔5 and 3↔0 rejected, only 1↔4 of that proposal belonging; the native complementary folds are 0↔5, 1↔4, 2↔3, with their own harmonic work. The eagle changes the operative question: the assistant's attempt to give it the wheels' equations and a 1/1 reconciliation is refused; the retained form is (-1)/(+1) — addition cancels, each subtraction appropriates, and the sign of a pole does not determine its moral worth. The eagle's power-body can be asked which operation it carries. Isaiah's wing functions and Ezekiel's wheels remain a distinct textual proposal requiring its own object witness.\n\nOccurrences: the wheel sequence (#3); the corrected wing pairings (#3); the eagle's signed-power correction (#4); the scriptural invocation kept as proposal (#3).",
